      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This paper examines Kant?s concept of hope within the framework of his theoretical and practical philosophy, focusing on the relation between morality, happiness, and the highest good. Hope is understood as a rationally justified expectation of happiness conditional upon moral action and is therefore subordinate to duty. While hope for happiness must not serve as the motive of moral action, it may legitimately accompany and sustain it. The transition from moral teleology to moral theology is interpreted as a reflective justification of rational hope, in which God?s existence is not theoretically proven but postulated as a condition of moral assurance and of the meaningfulness of moral striving in a world of finite, purposive beings. Hope is thus necessarily situated beyond the limits of theoretical cognition, and it is precisely through this limitation that it finds its proper place within moral teleology.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.2298/theo2601067m",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9816447bf9097c952b69b3fd7475083cda124fd9",
      "venue": "Theoria Beograd"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "f72fb234bc60cd81faf1250b713cd251319e8b7b",
      "title": "Kant and the argument from possibility",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Radenko Gurdeljevic",
          "authorId": "2419851475"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This paper examines Kant?s pre-critical argument from possibility, developed in The Only Possible Argument in Support of a Demonstration of the Existence of God (1763). In this work, Kant attempts to establish the existence of a necessary being on the basis of the very concept of internal possibility-conceivability without contradiction. The argument is based on a distinction between the formal element of possibility, grounded in the principle of non-contradiction, and the material element, which requires actual existence as a condition for anything to be thinkable. Without this material element, Kant argues, all possibility would be abolished. Since contingent beings cannot provide such a foundation, he concludes that a necessary being must exist as the ground of all internal possibility. The paper offers a systematic reconstruction of the argument and reassesses its philosophical significance. It argues that Kant?s early account of the relation between possibility and existence anticipates key problems that reappear-transformed-in his critical philosophy. In this way, the argument from possibility merits renewed attention, not merely as a historical curiosity, but as a significant step in the broader trajectory of Kant?s metaphysical development.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.2298/theo2601151g",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f72fb234bc60cd81faf1250b713cd251319e8b7b",
      "venue": "Theoria Beograd"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "c2ece59fe5478811a5f98af6e1f51e21c341c9e6",
      "title": "Self-censorship and the puzzle of publicity: Kant\u2019s political philosophy in the context of democracy/autocracy dichotomy",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "A. Soc",
          "authorId": "113693746"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This paper examines whether Kant?s practical philosophy is applicable to contemporary circumstances of politics. In the first section, I outline the challenge we face when trying to define these circumstances by pointing to the growing importance of recognizing the democracy/autocracy dichotomy. In the second section, I examine five prominent interpretations of morality-politics relationship within Kant?s philosophy. To show where such interpretations fall short, in the third section I outline the puzzle of publicity - i.e. the problem of applying Kant?s demand for publicity in contemporary autocratic societies (or, more broadly, oppressive circumstances) - by showing how it may lead to self-censorship, rather than autonomous use of reason. I suggest that to fully grasp the applicability of Kant?s views within contemporary complexity of political issues and debates, we need to carefully consider the hierarchy of Kant?s views by analyzing their transcendentality, i.e. by finding which tenet presents a necessary condition for other tenets. Thus, in the fourth section I offer a comprehensive picture of Kant?s key ideas in politics and ethics, and I show which ideas are fundamental and how all of them need to be transcendentally ordered for us to be able to apply Kant?s political philosophy today.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.2298/theo2601045s",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2ece59fe5478811a5f98af6e1f51e21c341c9e6",
      "venue": "Theoria Beograd"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "beb315eef7343b4bc6a96e67b6214d636919959d",
      "title": "What is man?",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Aleksandar Lukic",
          "authorId": "2335805330"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "In this paper, we explore the question of humanity, which has been one of the main questions throughout the history of philosophy. We approached the research through the teachings of three important thinkers - Plato, Kant and Scheler. The reason for choosing these philosophers is the importance they gave to anthropological problems on the one hand, and the continuity of the topic from ancient times to modern times. In Kant?s famous questions - what can I know, what should I do and what can I hope for, which refer to his three Critiques, the crucial anthropological question - what is man is contained. The basic difficulty that ?tormented? all thinkers is contained in the mystery of human nature, but also of nature in general, i.e. the unavailability of an indisputable answer to the question about being, which represents an almost insurmountable obstacle in philosophical research. In this work, we primarily tried to find some possible answers to the riddle of human existence through the thoughts of Plato, Kant and Scheler.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.2298/theo2601213l",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/beb315eef7343b4bc6a96e67b6214d636919959d",
      "venue": "Theoria Beograd"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "06a0ce29d8e664dda295a319ccb3cb35f024a89e",
      "title": "What\u2019s Really Wrong with Postmodernism?",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "D. Armitage",
          "authorId": "133813461"
        },
        {
          "name": "Danielle Haggerty",
          "authorId": "50705358"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "This paper offers a new critique of postmodernism by focusing on the inadequacy of postmodernism\u2019s underlying philosophy of mathematics, which we argue is more philosophically fundamental, and yet compatible with more common critiques of postmodernism, such as those exposing its self-referential incoherency. We define postmodernism as any philosophical system assuming a critique of the objective nature of rationality. Thus, firstly, this paper is devoted to a brief historical overview and argument for this understanding, with an emphasis on the implicit conception of mathematics (and reason) within it, which we argue is largely Kantian. We then turn to an analysis of Kant\u2019s theory of mathematics as it appears in the First Critique and the Prolegomena. Finally, we argue as follows: if Kant\u2019s theory of mathematics is incorrect, then postmodernism\u2019s \u201cmetaphysical\u201d pretensions as a critique of rationality, which both arise from and refer back to a Kantian view of mathematics, are bankrupt. The paper concludes by suggesting that the affirmation of intelligibility\u2019s reality implies a theistic metaphysics, uniting philosophical and theological reason.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.15290/rtk.2025.24.03",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/06a0ce29d8e664dda295a319ccb3cb35f024a89e",
      "venue": "Rocznik Teologii Katolickiej"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "b2f28f9543b7c7f31300a2b75ae29a17ef4160e1",
      "title": "Kant\u2019s two formulas of universal law and the relationship between right and morals in the Metaphysics of Morals",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Gehad Marcon Bark",
          "authorId": "2410662115"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This paper addresses the difference between Kant\u2019s two formulas of universal law in the Groundwork to the Metaphysics of Morals (1785) and in the Introduction to the Metaphysics of Morals (1797). The main argument is that the principle is formulated, in the last text, essentially as a principle of adjudication which is fully compatible with both internal and external lawgiving of ethical and juridical laws respectively. Given this new formulation, the principle is relieved from the strongly motivational content which characterizes it in the Groundwork, thus providing, alongside the conceptual framework developed in the Introduction to the Metaphysics of Morals, the textual basis needed for a better understanding of the moral grounds of obligation and duty in the realm of right according to Kantian thought.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.36311/2318-0501/2025.v13.e025008",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b2f28f9543b7c7f31300a2b75ae29a17ef4160e1",
      "venue": "Estudos Kantianos [EK]"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "9eddde45939a26709e4a8cd7f3f424edf62dc885",
      "title": "Kant on Utopia",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "K. Reinhardt",
          "authorId": "2053142741"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "\n Immanuel Kant's The Dispute between the Faculties (1798) contains a footnote referencing four utopian states \u2014 Atlantis, Utopia, Oceana, and Severambia. This passage has largely been overlooked in Kantian scholarship. This paper revisits this neglected passage to explore Kant's engagement with utopian literature and its implications for his philosophy of history and political thought. The footnote, though brief, sheds light on Kant's views regarding the feasibility of ideal states and their connection to revolutionary change. It reveals Kant's nuanced perspective on the role of utopian visions in shaping political ideals and the duties of individuals and governments in realizing such ideals. While Kant acknowledges these utopias as \u201csweet dreams,\u201d he also questions their practicality and warns against revolutionary approaches to achieving them. This paper examines how Kant's references to these utopian constructs reflect his broader philosophical concerns about progress and reform, emphasizing that progress must occur through gradual, lawful reform rather than through revolutionary upheaval. By analyzing Kant's treatment of these literary fictions, the paper aims to illuminate his complex relationship with utopian thought and its place within his broader theoretical framework.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1111/ejop.70044",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9eddde45939a26709e4a8cd7f3f424edf62dc885",
      "venue": "European Journal of Philosophy"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "779caf1b4edcb7d99ebe6d5b7d4d21eaed6d0edd",
      "title": "Why even a rational devil could be righteous under a Kantian account of right?",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Gehad Marcon Bark",
          "authorId": "2125757789"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This paper focuses on Kant\u2019s Towards Perpetual Peace famous statement according to which the problem of the State can be solved even for a race of devils. Its first aim is to show that coercion exerted via positive laws is pivotal to the understanding of Kant\u2019s main thesis regarding this nation of devils and the correspondent accomplishment of reason\u2019s ends through the \u201cmechanical course of nature\u201d (ZeF, AA 08, p. 367). According to this reviewed version of Kant\u2019s hypothesis, however, by refraining from violating the laws of a republican State out of self-interest, a devil would have to be taken as genuinely righteous according to rational principles. To make sense of this statement, Kant\u2019s hypothesis shall be developed and interpreted as coherently enclosing a general thesis regarding the normativity of right and its source on the external use of free choice independently of each agent\u2019s moral virtue.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.5380/dp.v22i3.100681",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/779caf1b4edcb7d99ebe6d5b7d4d21eaed6d0edd",
      "venue": "DoisPontos"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "19ef3e62702ed9bafd19785b9e43680be602c8e0",
      "title": "Myth and Truth about Kant\u2019s \u201cFormalism\u201d, or This Obscure Form of a Maxim",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Andrey Sudakov",
          "authorId": "2348394518"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The article attempts to problematize the grounds for Hegel\u2019s criticism of Kant\u2019s ethicsas \u201cformalism\u201d, which still remains a philosophical commonplace. For Hegel, Kant\u2019s position is the point of view of abstract morality, on which the rational principle of the good is the prin\u00adciple of the formal accordance of reason with itself, the law of self-reliance of the good as conscience; an essential property of Kantian morality is the requirement to fulfill duty for its own sake, and at the same time the allegedimpossibility to deduce any specific definitions of this duty, a system of private moralobligations. Hegel, followed by his disciples and fol\u00adlowers, concluded from this thatKantian ethics, as an ethics of the pure form of maxims, stopped at the ideal of goodwill, not proceeding \u2013 and not even having a logical possibility to proceed \u2013 to the actuality of the good. It turns out, however, that such criticism is based on a sort of a misunderstanding: on the identification of Kant\u2019s universal legislative form of a maximwith the logical form of universality inherent in the law of nature. Although the letterof Kant\u2019s definitions sometimes gives some cause for such an identification, upon the whole it distorts the view on Kant\u2019s ethics, in which the legislation of reason is not a logi\u00adcal, but a practical, i.e. freedom-mediated determination, and therefore the form of the princi\u00adple of will, which claims to participate in this legislation, is also a practicalform. If the mat\u00adter of the will is its object, the logical form of the maxim is the form of a system of means to achieve the object, the practical form of the maxim is a mode of self-determination to ac\u00adtions that achieve the object. The practical form of the maximconcerns the entire use of free\u00addom, therefore there can be only one legitimate practicalform of the maxim; however, this becomes clear only at the level of Kant\u2019s philosophyof moral religion. It is the practical form of the maxim of every action that can and must be legislative in Kant\u2019s ethics, i.e. the legiti\u00admate determining ground for the acts of freedom; the logical form of the maxim is practically incapable to determine anything practically. Only with this understanding of the matter is it possible to overcome the myth of Kantian \u201cformalism\u201d, at the same time retaining the valid and philosophicallyfruitful meaning of the concept of the legislative form of maxims.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.21146/2074-4870-2025-25-2-68-82",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/19ef3e62702ed9bafd19785b9e43680be602c8e0",
      "venue": "Ethical Thought"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "0bcd2baa40d6e227f0a8a7b5f72914e9325500bf",
      "title": "Validity and Reason",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Christian Krijnen",
          "authorId": "2317650046"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Abstract The problem of validity is the fundamental problem of philosophy. In this, transcendental and speculative idealism agree. Concerning the nature of the method to solve this problem, however, transcendental and speculative idealism diverge. Krijnen holds that due to its method, transcendental idealism fails to produce true self-knowledge of reason. In considering itself, thought forgets itself, which leads to the issue of externalitiy as an intrinsic feature of transcendental idealism. Krijnen illustrates this methodically induced externality by scrutinizing some doctrines of transcendental philosophy, placing particular emphasis on the contribution of the later generation of neo-Kantians like Bauch, Cassirer, and Cohn, but finally also on Fichte. The scrutinzed doctrines concerns the much-debated problem of the beginning of philosophy, the highly praised neo-Kantian concept of the concept as a function, and the self-destruction of the concept.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1515/jtph-2025-0022",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0bcd2baa40d6e227f0a8a7b5f72914e9325500bf",
      "venue": "Journal of Transcendental Philosophy"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "12f93c60e5a0cfa849106c65dc72a48089993235",
      "title": "Rationalizing and social irrationality from a Kantian perspective",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Eduardo De Oliveira da Costa",
          "authorId": "2419677610"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The present paper addresses the relation between, on the one hand, Kant\u2019s concept of rationalizing  and, on the other hand, the concept and phenomenon of social irrationality understood in a Kantian perspective. More specifically, this work argues that one can see in Kant\u2019s concept of rationalizing, which is understood by Kant as a form of irrationality, a source of social irrationality and, therefore, a conceptual tool that can help us to address the phenomenon of social irrationality in our society. To accomplish task, this paper is divided in four sections: an introduction (1), a definition of the concepts of irrationality and social irrationality (2), an explanation of the essential characteristics of Kant\u2019s concept of rationalizing as one can find in Kant\u2019s works and in the interpreters (3), and a demonstration of the usefulness of Kant\u2019s concept of rationalizing for understanding the sources of social irrationality in our society (4).",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.5380/dp.v22i3.100711",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/12f93c60e5a0cfa849106c65dc72a48089993235",
      "venue": "DoisPontos"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "b8d102b5a418588e5dc22ff2773611e8f5bb7425",
      "title": "Methodological Naturalism for Philosophy: Insights from Wittgenstein",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "A. Rakhmanin",
          "authorId": "2368168569"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Abstract This paper seeks to contribute to the ongoing debates about methodological naturalism in philosophy by examining the interpretations of Wittgenstein as a naturalist philosopher. Through analyzing these interpretations, I characterize Wittgenstein\u2019s position as a rare kind of naturalism independent of ontological or metaphysical claims. I show that what makes Wittgenstein\u2019s naturalism methodological is its metaphilosophical thrust, which I exemplify in two interrelated features of Wittgenstein\u2019s thought. First, the idea of philosophy\u2019s radical autonomy prevents philosophy from committing itself to any metaphysical thesis. Second, the emphasis that Wittgenstein places on the descriptiveness of philosophy makes \u201cgeneral facts of nature\u201d not the material of observation but the hinges of philosophy. Naturalism in Wittgenstein\u2019s thought, I argue, is the precondition for philosophy as opposed to science. Since Wittgenstein\u2019s naturalism is metaphilosophical, it is methodological: it prevents a philosopher from adopting the supernatural positions embodied in metaphysical theses. Thus, the proposed interpretation not only refines dominant views of Wittgenstein\u2019s naturalism as liberal or liberating, but also adds to the diversity of naturalistic positions in philosophy.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1515/sats-2025-0005",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8d102b5a418588e5dc22ff2773611e8f5bb7425",
      "venue": "Sats: northern european journal of philosophy"
    },
