    {
      "paperId": "505e564d4d71ac2511a08585d806d6c34b6ec042",
      "title": "A liking arising from displeasure - Kant\u2019s category of the sublime and the force of the subject\u2019s powerlessness",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Iva Draskic-Vicanovic",
          "authorId": "1404839780"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This text explores the role of the sublime in Kant?s transcendental philosophy. The author argues that the sublime is a key concept, present even in Kant?s pre-critical phase, and later gaining deeper significance beyond aesthetics within his critical system. The focus is on a transcendental analysis of the faculties involved in judgments of the sublime-imagination and reason-and their moral implications. Central to the argument is the notion of ?pleasure through displeasure,? where the imagination?s failure to grasp the absolute evokes a sense of inner defeat. Yet this failure awakens the subject?s awareness of its own inner strength-an indomitable power that resists all forces of nature. Thus, the sublime becomes a gateway to realizing the boundless moral potential of the self.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.2298/theo2601093d",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/505e564d4d71ac2511a08585d806d6c34b6ec042",
      "venue": "Theoria Beograd"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "9769cc61f5d3fab4b7df9a656f892fbb51350335",
      "title": "Love, Goodness and Moral Understanding: Two Reflections",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Salla Aldrin Salskov",
          "authorId": "1919664648"
        },
        {
          "name": "Philip Strammer",
          "authorId": "2234763303"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The article examines the relation between love and moral understanding in the context of post-Wittgensteinian ethics via two examples, the Biblical parable of the Good Samaritan and Raimond Gaita\u2019s autobiographical account of an encounter with a loving nun. The article suggests that the examples are best understood as teachings about love as expressive of moral understanding and speak of love thus understood in terms of a wholehearted responsiveness to others \u2013 as wholeheartedness. While both examples teach a similar lesson, they emphasise different aspects: whereas the discussion of the Biblical parable focuses on the moral understanding involved in being loving towards another, Gaita\u2019s anecdote foregrounds the question of what it is like to witness and understand such love. Our discussion shows that philosophy often overlooks the moral significance of love by rationalising morality. Whereas rational conceptions of morality overemphasise its practical and socio-cultural dimensions, the moral understanding that finds expression in love illustrates the radically personal nature of moral concerns. Thus, it is suggested that love is not merely an aspect of morality but lies at its very heart.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.15845/nwr.si2026.3788",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9769cc61f5d3fab4b7df9a656f892fbb51350335",
      "venue": "Nordic Wittgenstein Review"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "7ae0431c089825ef1b05b3fa6b17afd044ff2086",
      "title": "Arguing with Kant in the Postcolonial Middle East",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Michael Frey",
          "authorId": "2056524551"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "\n \n This article examines the first Arabic translations of Immanuel Kant\u2019s\n Critique of Pure Reason\n (1965) and\n Critique of Practical Reason\n (1966) in light of the intellectual journey of their translator, A\u1e25mad al-\u0160ayb\u0101n\u012b (1923\u201395). It argues that, shaped by experiences of repression, exile, and a search for identity, al-\u0160ayb\u0101n\u012b developed in his own writings a core moral concern centered on human dignity, freedom, and rationalism\u2014values that he perceived to be upheld by Islam and threatened by what he calls \u201cmaterialism\u201d. As the prefaces to his translations show, Kant helped him synthesize these beliefs, including his religious convictions. By offering a historically grounded perspective on Kant as a global thinker that goes beyond parochial Eurocentric truth claims, this article also draws attention to a philosophical engagement with the political and social changes of the postcolonial Middle East that evades the ideological framing prevalent in the intellectual historiography of that era.\n",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1163/15700607-20262019",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7ae0431c089825ef1b05b3fa6b17afd044ff2086",
      "venue": "Die Welt des Islams"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "73d39b5440b7ced5cd71ffae52f9ed04a60b755a",
      "title": "The Problem of Methodological Dogmatism: The Curious Case of Kant on Race",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Zachary Vereb",
          "authorId": "117290988"
        },
        {
          "name": "William A. B. Parkhurst",
          "authorId": "2406160878"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "\n \n We argue that scholars involved in debates on Kant\u2019s writings on race and racism are deeply entangled with a tacit methodological debate about the use of a \u2018priority principle\u2019. We identify three variants of the priority principle in Kant scholarship. To illustrate, we focus on interpretations of Kant\u2019s\n Physical Geography\n . The methodological approaches we analyse offer three\n opposite and mutually exclusive\n interpretative recommendations. We articulate a taxonomy of methods commonly employed and suggest that focusing on individual texts reveals value-laden methodological assumptions guiding the debate. To address substantive issues surrounding Kant\u2019s raciology, we suggest commentators should carefully justify their methodological choices.\n",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1017/s1369415425101180",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/73d39b5440b7ced5cd71ffae52f9ed04a60b755a",
