    {
      "paperId": "377c3327361144dac5c9a46e7597fe30879fa8ee",
      "title": "Ontic Structural Realism and the Case of the Missing Kantian Residue",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "R. van der Merwe",
          "authorId": "1814605477"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "As the name suggests, Ontic Structural Realism (OSR) entails the claim that structure is all there is. However, several critics have argued that OSR\u2019s ontology is incomplete. There must be something ontologically significant beyond structure. I will suggest an ontology for these critics, one that invokes what Ladyman and Ross call \u201cKantian residue\u201d. In doing so, I modify Rae Langton\u2019s Kantian humility thesis to incorporate some extra-structural noumenal \u201csomething=x\u201d (as Kant puts it). This involves positing (a) that a mysterious something=x exists and (b) that something=x is responsible for structure (specifically structure\u2019s non-arbitrary properties). Responsibility is a modal relation that is potentially equivalent to some asymmetric relation (causation, explanation, determination, grounding, or the like). Regarding the nature of something=x, I argue in deflationary spirit that we can only know that it is that which is responsible for structure.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.36446/af.e1202",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/377c3327361144dac5c9a46e7597fe30879fa8ee",
      "venue": "An\u00e1lisis filos\u00f3fico"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "375a8983865c15ce8a52726a56971a09e9f539fb",
      "title": "Kant on Citizenship and Poverty",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Nicholas Vrousalis",
          "authorId": "2372766444"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "According to Kant, citizenship amounts to freedom (Freiheit), equality (Gleichheit), and civil self-sufficiency (Selbst\u00e4ndigkeit). This Element provides a unifying interpretation of these three elements. Vrousalis argues that Kant affirms the idea of interdependent independence: in the just society, citizens have independent use of their interdependent rightful powers. Kant therefore thinks of the modern state as a system of cooperative production, in which reciprocal entitlements to one another's labour carry a justificatory burden. The empirical form of that ideal is a republic of economically independent commodity producers. It follows that citizenship and poverty, for Kant, are inextricably connected. Vrousalis explains how Kant's arguments anticipate Hegel's discussion of the division of labour, Marx's account of alienated labour, and Rawls' defence of a well-ordered society. This title is also available as Open Access on Cambridge Core.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1017/9781009671323",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/375a8983865c15ce8a52726a56971a09e9f539fb",
      "venue": ""
    },
    {
      "paperId": "b14f287e6e0b197c2d7d2580e9d72e499b0fd4ed",
      "title": "Encyclopedia of Early Russian Kant-Studies. Review on: Kruglov A.N. Kant\u2019s Philosophy in Russia. Kaliningrad: Immanuel Kant Baltic Federal University Press, 2024. 795 p.",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Andrei Sudakov",
          "authorId": "2410424899"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The review focuses on A.N. Kruglov\u2019s monograph \u201cKant\u2019s Philosophy in Russia\u201d, which is a com\u00adprehensive study of early Russian Kantianism. The reviewer notes that Kruglov has undertaken an extensive reconstruction of the reception of Kant\u2019s philosophy in Russia during the late 18th and mid-19th centuries, drawing on a wide range of archival and published sources. Special attention is paid to the first Russian translations of Kant\u2019s works, the role of universities and theological acad\u00ademies in the dissemination of critical philosophy, as well as the contributions of key figures such as Melman, Odoevsky, Solovyov, Lopatin, and Trubetskoy. The book also explores the interpreta\u00adtion of Kant\u2019s ideas by Russian Marxists. The reviewer highlights the impressive scope of the ma\u00adterial covered in the book, which encompasses not only philosophical, but also cultural and histori\u00adcal contexts that reveal how Kant\u2019s ideas were understood, reinterpreted, and transformed within the Russian intellectual tradition. At the same time, the author acknowledges the methodological challenges inherent in a project of such an encyclopedic scale: limited analysis of individual thinkers, uneven source material, and ambiguity in the criteria for including works within the Rus\u00adsian Kantian corpus. Despite these difficulties, Kruglov\u2019s work is recognized as a valuable contri\u00adbution \u2013 an ambitious and well-researched attempt to systematize the phenomenon of \u201cKant in Russia\u201d, opening up new perspectives for future studies on the history of Russian reception of critical philosophy.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.21146/2949-3102-2025-3-3-116-127",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b14f287e6e0b197c2d7d2580e9d72e499b0fd4ed",
      "venue": "Otechestvennaya Filosofiya"
    },
