          "name": "Joris van Gorkom",
          "authorId": "1662751143"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Abstract Despite his academic position and contributions to one of the most respected German encyclopedias, Ludwig Emil Cichorius (1770\u20131829) remains an unknown figure in German intellectual history. This article explores Cichorius\u2019 publications on the topic of race, highlighting Kant\u2019s influence on 19th-century thought through Cichorius\u2019 contributions to the Conversations-Lexikon of Renatus Gotthelf L\u00f6bel (1767\u20131799), Christian Wilhelm Franke (1765\u20131831), and Friedrich Arnold Brockhaus (1772\u20131823). These contributions to the lexicon included distinct entries on the \u201craces of human beings\u201d and \u201craces of animals,\u201d where he engaged with Kantian ideas. He underscored the significance of unfailingly inherited traits over superficial characteristics in classifying races.",
      "citationCount": 0,
